---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import tech from '../data/tech.json';
---
<Base title="The tech">
  <h1>The technology: what it collects, what it infers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A camera produces a plate, a timestamp, and a location. The software turns those reads
    into associations, patterns of life, and predictions. Below: what the systems already do,
    then every provider — click through for their hardware, model by model, and their platform.
  </p>

  <h2>What the software already does — documented</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Use case</th><th>The shipping feature</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Map who travels with whom</td><td><a href="/tech/flock">Flock Convoy Analysis</a>; <a href="/tech/motorola">Vigilant Associate Analysis</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>List everyone at a sensitive location</td><td><a href="/tech/motorola">Vigilant Stakeout</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Predict where a vehicle will be</td><td><a href="/tech/motorola">Vigilant Locate Analysis</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Track a driver across states</td><td><a href="/tech/flock">Flock Multi-Geo</a> (the Texas abortion search touched 83,000 cameras)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Turn a plate into a person</td><td><a href="/tech/flock">Flock Nova</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fingerprint the devices in your car</td><td><a href="/tech/leonardo">Leonardo SignalTrace</a>; <a href="/tech/jenoptik">Jenoptik TraffiCatch</a> (deployed since ~2019)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Screen for immigration status via local alerts</td><td>NCIC hotlist ingestion (ICE's Immigration Violator File — Flock, Axon, Vigilant, ELSAG)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stalk an ex</td><td>Any of them — ~50 documented US cases; the audit log is the only thing that caught anyone</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The providers</h2>
  <p class="lede">Each page covers one company's platform and its hardware — radios, sensors, and certifications, model by model.</p>
  <div class="grid">
    {tech.vendors.map((v) => (
      <a class="vcard" href={`/tech/${v.slug}`}>
        <h3>{v.name}</h3>
        <span class="badge">{v.radioBadge}</span>
        <p>{v.summary}</p>
        <span class="go">Hardware + platform →</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section class="antenna">
    <h2>⚠ The antenna problem: it stopped being "just plates"</h2>
    <p>
      Two ALPR vendors now sell plate readers that also capture the wireless signals inside
      your car. <strong><a href="/tech/jenoptik">Jenoptik's TraffiCatch</a></strong> — deployed in
      Texas border counties since ~2019 on DHS grant money — detects Bluetooth addresses from
      phones, earbuds, and car systems and merges them with the plate read.
      <strong><a href="/tech/leonardo">Leonardo's SignalTrace</a></strong> (announced 2026) markets
      "electronic fingerprints": identifying "groups of consumer electronic devices that
      routinely travel together," with or without a readable plate. Your phone randomizes its
      identifiers; your earbuds, smartwatch, car head unit, and tire-pressure sensors mostly
      don't — and the <em>combination</em> of devices you carry is a signature even when each
      one behaves.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sharper fact: on most modern networked ALPR hardware, <strong>the radio is already
      installed</strong>. <a href="/tech/flock">Flock's Falcon</a> contains an active Wi-Fi +
      Bluetooth module (18,000+ of its own hotspots are visible on public wardriving
      databases); <a href="/tech/motorola">Motorola's L6Q</a> and <a href="/tech/axon">Axon's
      Outpost</a> ship certified radio stacks. None of them sniff today — but for those
      platforms, device tracking is a software update plus a legal decision, not a hardware
      purchase. <a href="/tech/genetec">Genetec's cameras</a> are the cert-verified exception:
      no Wi-Fi or Bluetooth on any model.
    </p>
    <p>
      And no retention statute in the <a href="/rules">rules comparison</a> mentions device
      signals. Every rule ever written about this technology regulates the plate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="analysis">
    <h2>Analysis: where the documented capabilities point</h2>
    <p class="disclaimer">
      This section is analysis, not reporting — written from professional experience
      building AI systems. Every item extends a capability the vendor documents; none
      asserts a system is doing this today.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The borrowed car doesn't help you.</strong> SignalTrace fingerprints the devices, not the vehicle, and works "with or without" a plate read — the vendor's framing, taken one honest step: switch cars, keep your phone and watch, and you're the same fingerprint on a different chassis.</li>
      <li><strong>Four devices that always move together are an inference engine's favourite fact.</strong> "Devices that routinely travel together" (Leonardo's words) is a household, a crew, a carpool, a congregation — association mapping that never needed a licence plate. The plate-side version already ships as Convoy Analysis.</li>
      <li><strong>Escalation is the roadmap, not a slippery slope.</strong> Flock already launches drones off camera hits; SoundThinking already chains gunshot → plate → person-records in one workflow. Sensor event to person file is the documented product direction of the two most aggressive vendors.</li>
      <li><strong>The least-regulated layer is growing fastest.</strong> Passive receivers carry no certification trail, and no retention rule in our <a href="/rules">rules comparison</a> mentions device signals. The inference layer's newest input sits in the emptiest part of the rulebook.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    .table-wrap a { color: var(--toll); }
    .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(17rem, 1fr)); gap: 0.8rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
    .vcard {
      display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.35rem; text-decoration: none;
      background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    }
    .vcard:hover { border-color: var(--toll); }
    .vcard h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 0; }
    .badge {
      align-self: flex-start; font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700;
      padding: 0.1rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 999px;
      background: rgba(255,181,77,0.14); color: var(--fleet);
    }
    .vcard p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.84rem; flex: 1; }
    .go { color: var(--toll); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; }
    .antenna { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--police); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
    .antenna h2 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; color: var(--police); }
    .antenna p { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text); margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .antenna a { color: var(--toll); }
    .analysis { border: 1px solid var(--toll); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; background: var(--panel); }
    .analysis h2 { margin-top: 0; color: var(--toll); }
    .disclaimer { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.84rem; margin-bottom: 0.8rem; font-style: italic; }
    .analysis ul { padding-left: 1.15rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .analysis li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .analysis a { color: var(--toll); }
  </style>
</Base>
